this is the Aedes aegypti mosquito it's  the main mosquito that carries Zika  developing a Zika vaccine could take a  decade so right now officials only tool  is to control mosquitoes the Zika virus  was first identified in Uganda in 1947  and caused relatively mild flu-like  symptoms in May of last year  Zika appeared in Brazil since then  Brazil has seen an alarming increase in  babies born with abnormally small heads  a birth defect called microcephaly in  just 10 months Zika spread from Brazil  to Central America Mexico and the  Caribbean and it's expected to reach  almost every country of the Americas  several cases have already been reported  in the u.s. the Aedes is the same  mosquito that transmits dengue and  chikungunya it transmits Zika by biting  someone who has the virus and then  biting another human the virus then  moves to new areas where mosquitoes  hitch a ride on travellers or cargo or  more often when patients infected at one  place go to a new territory and are  bitten by mosquitoes there there's also  some evidence that it can spread through  sexual content but it's the possible  transmission for mother to fetus through  the placental barrier that has health  officials alarmed some countries are  telling women to put off getting  pregnant for months or even years  only about one in five people infected  show symptoms for those who do get sick  the illness usually isn't severe lasts  about a week however researchers are  also investigating a connection to Keyon  Barea syndrome a nerve disorder that can  cause paralysis what will it take to  stop see other than traditional methods  to control the sceetos one strategy  that's currently being tested is  genetically modified folks the Brazilian  government has approved the expansion of  a pilot program to release male  mosquitoes that have been modified so  that when they breathe wild females the  offspring die early studies have shown  high success rates but opponents fear  unknown consequences the technology is  also in its infancy as more people  travel there's a higher chance of  mosquito-borne diseases like Zika is  spreading to new places ultimately  authorities will need better weapons  against bugs
 

